                <pb facs="0659" n="647" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg647"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">832.24/278: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Brazil</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Burdett</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de
                                Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-12-06T15:00:00-03:00">December 6, 1940—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 5:37 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>636. For the Under Secretary. Reference Embassy’s telegram No. 632, December 5, 2
                    p.m. Aranha received today a telegram from his Ambassador at London saying that
                    the Foreign Office informed him that as the British Government desires to
                    maintain good relations and cooperation with Brazil and, having in mind at all
                    times the solidarity existing between Brazil and the United States, it will
                    search for a formula for a satisfactory settlement of the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Siqueira Campos</hi> case.</p>
                <p>A definite decision will be made by the Council of Ministers. The formula being
                    sought is one which will permit a settlement without creating any precedents.
                    The Ambassador thinks that a settlement will be reached within 2 or 3 days.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Burdett</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
